Proposal: Mobility Project

**DATE:**			December 12, 2025 

**SUBMITTED TO**:	QCells

**PROJECT:**		Mobility Project

**SUBMITTED BY**:	Andy Johnston P.E.

			Johnston Engineering, PLLC

Johnston Engineering is pleased to submit this proposal for mechanical engineering design of two concepts for QCells Tech Center: a Frontloader Attachment / End Effector, and a Off Road Construction Trailer. 

# Intro: 

Johnston Engineering (JE) is a mechanical engineering firm located in Spokane, Washington **specializing in the design, analysis, integration, build, and testing of first-of-a-kind systems**. In operation since 2015, JE brings over 250 years of combined engineering experience across a team of 23, with deep expertise in product development, system integration and advanced engineering solutions. Our work spans critical material recovery systems, industrial machinery, as well as clean technology sectors including carbon capture, hydrogen, nuclear systems, solar, and long-duration energy storage.

**At JE, our designers are also ****your ****analysts,** utilizing physics-based simulations of thermal, structural, and fluid-flow analysis to optimize and validate designs before we build systems. This closely integrated coupling of design and analysis ensures that performance is validated before fabrication begins, preventing part failures and accelerating development. Using SolidWorks 3D CAD and ANSYS Finite Element Analysis (FEA), our team excels in component modeling, full system integration, and stress analysis.

**At JE ,our designers are also your builders**. The same engineers who model and analyze the system are also in charge of fabrication oversight, procurement, assembly, and testing. This continuity produces engineers with a uniquely deep understanding of the design intent, system behavior, and how the system is installed in the real world.

To date, JE has completed more than 190 first-of-a-kind engineering projects for various clients and 29 projects with the Department of Energy (DOE). Our focus on FOAK development, combined with our design-analysis-build-test approach, makes Johnston Engineering an ideal partner to develop QCells Mobility project.

# Scope:

Johnston Engineering will provide mechanical engineering design and analysis services to develop two equipment systems under the QCells Mobility Project: (1) a custom **Frontloader Attachment** and (2) an **Off-Road Construction Trailer**. Both systems are intended to support utility-scale solar module installation operations with improved efficiency, operator safety, and material protection.

Johnston Engineering will deliver complete 3D CAD models, 2D fabrication-ready drawings, and accompanying static structural analyses for both systems. All deliverables are targeted for completion by the **e****nd of ****February 2026** in accordance with QCells’ project schedule. This proposal covers design and testing support only, excluding prototype fabrication at this stage. 

# Design Approach: 

# The table below summarizes the target capabilities for each system and Johnston Engineering’s corresponding design approach. 

The figures below are intended to convey a notional system layout and design approach.

| **Concept** | **Requested Capability** | **Design Approach/ Additional Notes** |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Front Loader | Grippers engage torque tube | Using parallel gripper design |
|  | Vertical adjustment on both sides for fine adjustment of slope | Using Rotary design to keep grippers in-line with each other and to bring horizontal when one side of loader is lower than other, such as one wheel in a hole |
|  | Horizontal adjustment from linear hydraulic actuators for N/S fine-tuning | Designing in side-shift similar to fork-lift design – approximately +/- 6” allowance on either side for fine adjustment |
|  | Independent latera adjustment for E/W tuning | We feel this is redundant and can be done with the front-end loader which simplifies the design and cost |
|  | Fine adjustment Tile from helical hydraulic actuator | Design will include rotary adjustment from loader attachment to end effector using hydraulic actuator |
|  | Attach to standard pins on front loader | Understood. – Qcells provides JE with dimensional / pictorial drawing of attachment point to design from |
| Off Road Construction Cart | Individual legs hydraulically | It may be simpler to have a pinned leg in the up position and pulling the pin allows the leg to go nearly flat on the trailer |

*Figure 1** – Front Loader **End-Effector** with **R**otational **S**ide **H**ill **L**eveling *

*Figure 2** – **P**arallel **G**ripper **A**ssembly *

**Phase1: Frontloader Attachment Design**

**Key Tasks:**** **Design of a custom front loader attachment capable of safely and repeatedly lifting pre-assembled solar module, rail, and torque tube sections for utility-scale solar applications. The attachment should support reliable daily use, minimize material damage, and promote operator efficiency.

- Requirements Definition (10 hours)

- Collaborate with QCells to define final interface geometry, lifting tolerances, and operational safety constraints.

- 3D CAD Development (100 hours)

- Design modular frame, attachment interfaces, and adjustable gripping/handling features.

- Concept iteration

- Review meetings with QCells

- Structural Analysis (30 hours)

- Perform static structural FEA to verify safe loading and stress distribution under operational conditions.

- S-N Curve based fatigue analysis on attachment end effector to validate field durability.

- Drafting Detailed Fabrication Drawings (20 hours)

- Generate 2D manufacturing drawings of complete assembly and all subcomponents.

**Phase2: Off-Road Construction Trailer Design**

Key Tasks:  Design of simple, manually operated support leg assembly to bolt to existing trailer 

- Requirements Definition (4 hours)

- Collaborate with QCells to define final interface geometry, lifting tolerances, and operational safety constraints.

- 3D CAD Development (16 hours)

- CAD design of trailer supports to mount to existing trailer

- Structural & Load Analysis (8 Hours)

- Simulate towing vertical, side and forward and aft loads using equivalent g-loading static structural analysis.  Client to identify desired g-loading.  

- Drafting Detailed Fabrication Drawings (8 hours)

- Generate 2D manufacturing drawings of complete assembly and all subcomponents.

**Deliverables: **

- 3D CAD models (SolidWorks and STEP formats) for both equipment systems

- 2D manufacturing drawings (PDF and/or native formats)

- Static structural analysis reports

**Exclusion****s****: **

- Hardware Quoting and Fabrication: This proposal covers design and analysis services only. Fabrication, assembly, or procurement of hardware components is excluded and may be scoped separately following design approval.

- Front-End Loader pin interface definition: QCells is responsible for providing the exact front-end loader pin interface specifications. Johnston Engineering will design the attachment interface based on the data provided.

# Cost: 

This proposal is based on a time and material (T&M) hourly rate of $205/hr. Johnston Engineering estimates 160 hours (**$32,800**) needed to complete the design and analysis of the Frontloader Attachment and 36 hours (**$7,380**) needed to complete the design and analysis of the Off-Road Construction Trailer Design. Costs will be incurred only for hours worked and materials purchased. The above estimates are provided for budgeting purposes. Historically, our estimates are accurate to +/- 20%, assuming no change in project scope. 

If QCells prefers a fixed fee arrangement, JE will provide all deliverables for **$48,216**. 

Our commitment is to deliver our services in the capacity that best supports the success of this project. Please do not hesitate to reach out with any questions or for further clarification on any aspects of this proposal. 

